  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value. It is 3 times steeper left to right, so overshoot shows there first: from about learning rate 0.34 the ball zig-zags, and above about 0.67 the swings grow.",
    "Each step moves the ball by −learning rate × (slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)) per direction: 2 at batch size 1, 0 at 64.",
    "Purple arrow: the next mini-batch step, the way the ball will move. Dark arrow: the step the full data would give. Both are drawn at 2 times their real length, using the same scale. A zero-length step is hidden.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6, the loss at the bowl's left and right rims (diverged). Path points off the bowl are drawn at its rim. The loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed. A new run uses the next noise values in that sequence. Reset replays the sequence from the start and clears both paths.",
    "While running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed. Changing a setting mid-run affects only the steps after the change."
  ]
}